export const C1={title:'Clinical laboratory professions, quality and instruments',hook:'About two-thirds of medical decisions are commonly said to depend on laboratory results, yet most patients never meet the people who produce them. Medical laboratory professionals work behind the scenes, turning a tube of blood or a cup of urine into numbers clinicians trust. That trust depends on trained people, regulated processes and the right instrument for each job.',objectives:['Describe the education and roles of medical laboratory scientists, technicians, assistants and phlebotomists.','Explain how CLIA regulates laboratory testing and test complexity.','Describe the pre-analytical, analytical and post-analytical phases and quality control.','Identify common laboratory instruments and equipment and their purposes.'],sections:[
['The laboratory team',[
'The clinical laboratory employs several professions. A **medical laboratory scientist (MLS)**, also called a medical technologist, typically holds a **bachelor’s degree** in medical laboratory science or a related science with clinical training, performs complex testing, troubleshoots instruments, correlates results and may supervise. A **medical laboratory technician (MLT)** usually holds an **associate degree** and performs routine to moderately complex testing. A **medical laboratory assistant** prepares and processes specimens and performs simple tests. A **phlebotomy technician**, who may have a high school diploma or GED plus phlebotomy training, collects blood specimens—the answer to the HOSA sample question. **Pathologists** are physicians who interpret tissue and laboratory findings and often direct laboratories. **Histotechnicians** prepare tissue slides, and **cytotechnologists** examine cells for cancer.',
'Many professionals hold certification from the **American Society for Clinical Pathology (ASCP) Board of Certification**, and some states require licensure. Laboratory work is organized into departments: **hematology**, **coagulation**, **clinical chemistry**, **immunology/serology**, **immunohematology (blood bank)**, **microbiology**, **urinalysis**, **parasitology** and **molecular diagnostics**.',
match('Match the laboratory role',[['Bachelor’s degree; performs complex testing and troubleshooting','Medical laboratory scientist'],['Associate degree; routine to moderately complex testing','Medical laboratory technician'],['High school diploma or GED plus training to collect blood','Phlebotomy technician'],['Physician who interprets tissue and directs the laboratory','Pathologist'],['Examines cells for signs of cancer','Cytotechnologist'],['Prepares and processes specimens and simple tests','Medical laboratory assistant']])]],
['Regulation and test complexity',[
'In the United States, the **Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments of 1988 (CLIA)** set quality standards for all laboratory testing on human specimens used for diagnosis or treatment, and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services administers the program. CLIA classifies tests by complexity. **Waived tests** are simple with low risk of error, such as glucose meters, urine pregnancy tests and some rapid strep tests, and can be done in physician offices with a certificate of waiver. **Moderate-complexity** and **high-complexity** tests require more qualified personnel, proficiency testing and quality control. Accrediting organizations such as the College of American Pathologists inspect laboratories, and **proficiency testing** sends unknown samples to labs to verify accuracy.']],
['Phases of testing and quality control',[
'Errors can happen at any stage. The **pre-analytical phase** includes the test order, patient identification, preparation (such as fasting), specimen collection, labeling, transport and processing; most laboratory errors occur here. The **analytical phase** is the testing itself, including instrument calibration and quality control. The **post-analytical phase** includes reporting, interpretation, critical-value notification and record keeping.',
'**Quality control (QC)** runs materials with known values alongside patient samples to confirm the system works; QC results are plotted on **Levey-Jennings charts** to spot shifts and trends. **Calibration** adjusts an instrument to standards. **Critical values** (panic values), such as a dangerously high potassium, must be reported immediately to the responsible clinician and documented, including who received the result and read-back confirmation. **Reference ranges** describe values found in healthy people and can differ by laboratory, age and sex.',
sort('Which testing phase?',['Pre-analytical','Analytical','Post-analytical'],[['Identifying the patient with two identifiers',0],['Labeling the tube at the bedside',0],['Centrifuging and separating serum',0],['Running quality control with patient samples',1],['Calibrating the chemistry analyzer',1],['Calling a critical potassium result to the nurse',2],['Releasing the report to the electronic record',2]])]],
['Instruments and equipment (HOSA Skill I)',[
'HOSA Skill I asks competitors to identify up to 15 instruments or photos by **name and purpose**. Group them by department to study. **Specimen collection**: tourniquet, single-use lancet, disposable needle holder with safety guard, Saf-T Wing blood collection set, safety syringes, plastic vacuum tubes, capillary collection vials, sterile vacuum tube for urine, urine sterile collecting straw, clean-catch urine collection kit, 24-hour urine specimen container, stool specimen container, culture swabs and transport tubes, and the **perianal paddle kit** for pinworm testing. **Processing and measurement**: clinical centrifuge, serological centrifuge, microhematocrit centrifuge with microhematocrit tubes and sealant pad, needleless transfer device, top-loading and analytical balances, pH meter and pH indicator strips, micropipettes, pipet aids, beakers, Erlenmeyer flasks, graduated cylinders, volumetric flasks, test tubes and transfer (Beral) pipettes.',
'**Hematology and coagulation**: manual differential cell counter, hemacytometer, slide staining rack, automatic slide stainer, coagulation instrument, point-of-care coagulation analyzer, platelet aggregation profiler and rapid latex agglutination test for D-dimer. **Urinalysis and chemistry**: refractometer and urinometer for specific gravity, urine reagent strips and strip reader, urine particle analyzer, Acetest tablets for ketones, chromatographic immunoassay for urine hCG and glucose meter. **Microbiology**: agar plates, inoculating loop, bacteriological incubator, candle jar (for increased carbon dioxide) and electric incinerator for sterilizing loops. **Microscopy**: binocular bright-field, transmission electron and scanning electron microscopes. **Safety and storage**: N95 respirator, acrylic shield, fume hood, emergency eye wash station, safety shower, tabletop autoclave and blood bank refrigerator.',
flip('Identify the instrument and its purpose',[['Refractometer','Measures urine specific gravity using refraction of light'],['Hemacytometer','Counting chamber for manual cell counts'],['Microhematocrit centrifuge','Spins capillary tubes to measure packed cell volume'],['Candle jar','Creates a carbon dioxide–enriched atmosphere for fastidious bacteria'],['Electric incinerator','Sterilizes inoculating loops without an open flame'],['Perianal paddle kit','Collects pinworm eggs from the perianal area'],['Acetest tablet','Detects ketones in urine or serum'],['Acrylic shield','Barrier protecting the worker from splashes'],['Fume hood','Ventilated enclosure that removes chemical vapors'],['Point-of-care coagulation analyzer','Measures PT/INR at the bedside from a fingerstick']])]],
['Careers and outlook',[
'The Bureau of Labor Statistics reports steady demand for clinical laboratory technologists and technicians, driven by an aging population and new diagnostic tests. Laboratory professionals work in hospitals, reference laboratories, physician offices, public health laboratories, blood banks, research and industry. The work suits people who enjoy science, precision and problem-solving and who are comfortable working with instruments and computers. Because results guide diagnosis and treatment, integrity matters: never report a result you are not confident in, and document every corrective action.',
kase('A critical value',{a:{text:'A potassium result comes back at 6.9 mmol/L, above the laboratory’s critical limit. QC for the run was acceptable. What should the laboratory professional do?',choices:[['Check the specimen for hemolysis or errors, then call the critical value promptly to the responsible caregiver','b','Correct. Verify quickly, then notify immediately.',true],['Release it to the electronic record and wait for someone to notice','b','Critical values require direct, immediate notification.',false]]},b:{text:'The nurse answers the call. What completes the notification?',choices:[['Ask the nurse to read back the result and document the name, time and read-back','c','Correct. Read-back and documentation close the loop.',true],['Hang up once the value is stated','c','Read-back confirms the result was heard correctly.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse reads back “potassium 6.9.” The laboratory professional documents the nurse’s name, the time and the read-back in the report.',end:true}})]],
["Specimen labeling and laboratory information systems",["Every specimen must be labeled **at the patient’s side** immediately after collection with at least **two patient identifiers** (such as full name and date of birth or medical record number), the date and time of collection and the collector’s initials. Unlabeled or mislabeled specimens are rejected because a wrong-patient result can be dangerous. Other common **rejection criteria** include hemolysis for certain tests, clotted samples in anticoagulant tubes, underfilled light blue tubes, wrong tube type, leaking containers and specimens too old to test.","The **laboratory information system (LIS)** receives orders, prints barcode labels, tracks specimens, sends results from analyzers, applies reference ranges and flags, and transmits reports to the electronic health record. Barcodes reduce transcription errors. Laboratory professionals protect patient privacy under HIPAA, accessing results only for work purposes. A **chain of custody** form documents every person who handles specimens used for legal purposes, such as drug testing or forensic samples."]]
],takeaways:['MLS (bachelor’s), MLT (associate), lab assistant and phlebotomy technician (HS diploma plus training) form the lab team.','CLIA (1988) classifies tests as waived, moderate or high complexity; most errors are pre-analytical.','QC, calibration and immediate critical-value calls with read-back protect patients; know every Skill I instrument’s name and purpose.'],terms:[['CLIA','Federal standards for laboratory testing on human specimens.'],['Quality control','Testing known materials to verify the testing system works.'],['Critical value','A result so abnormal it must be reported immediately.']],mcqs:[q('What is the title for a person with a high school diploma or GED trained to collect blood specimens?','Phlebotomy technician','Medical laboratory assistant','Laboratory manager','Medical laboratory technician','This is the HOSA sample question.'),q('A medical laboratory scientist typically holds a:','Bachelor’s degree','High school diploma only','Associate degree only','Medical degree','MLTs usually hold associate degrees.'),q('Which law sets quality standards for U.S. clinical laboratory testing?','CLIA','HIPAA','OSHA Act','EMTALA','CLIA was passed in 1988.'),q('A glucose meter test in a physician office is usually classified as:','Waived','High complexity','Moderate complexity','Research only','Waived tests are simple and low-risk.'),q('In which phase do most laboratory errors occur?','Pre-analytical','Analytical','Post-analytical','Proficiency testing','Identification, collection and labeling cause most errors.'),q('Which instrument measures urine specific gravity?','Refractometer','Hemacytometer','Fume hood','pH meter','A urinometer can also be used.'),q('A candle jar is used to:','Provide a carbon dioxide–enriched atmosphere for culture','Sterilize loops','Measure pH','Store blood','Fastidious bacteria need extra CO₂.'),q('After calling a critical value, the caller should:','Have the receiver read back the result and document it','Delete the result','Wait until the next shift','Email the result only','Read-back ensures accuracy.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the roles on a clinical laboratory team, explain how CLIA and quality control protect patients across the three phases of testing, and identify four instruments from the HOSA list with their purposes.',rubric:[['Laboratory roles and education',['scientist','technician','phlebotom','bachelor','associate']],['CLIA and complexity',['clia','waived','complexity','proficiency']],['Three phases and QC',['pre-analytical','analytical','post-analytical','quality control','critical']],['Four instruments with purposes',['refractometer','hemacytometer','centrifuge','incubator','autoclave','microscope','candle jar']]],model:'Medical laboratory scientists, usually with bachelor’s degrees, perform complex testing and troubleshooting; technicians with associate degrees perform routine testing; laboratory assistants process specimens; and phlebotomy technicians collect blood. CLIA sets national standards and classifies tests as waived, moderate or high complexity, requiring qualified staff, quality control and proficiency testing. In the pre-analytical phase, correct identification, labeling and handling prevent most errors; in the analytical phase, calibration and quality control on Levey-Jennings charts verify accuracy; in the post-analytical phase, results are reported and critical values are called immediately with read-back. A refractometer measures urine specific gravity, a hemacytometer is used for manual cell counts, a microhematocrit centrifuge measures packed cell volume and a bacteriological incubator grows cultures at body temperature.'},sources:[RULES,CMS_CLIA,ASCP,BLS_LAB]};

export const C2={title:'Biological safety, infection control and PPE',hook:'A laboratory worker handles hundreds of blood, urine and culture specimens every day, any of which could carry a bloodborne pathogen or contagious organism. HOSA’s Skill II tests a sequence that protects workers everywhere in health care: wash hands, put on PPE in the right order, and take it off without contaminating yourself.',objectives:['Explain the chain of infection and standard and transmission-based precautions.','Perform hand hygiene and don and doff PPE in the correct order (Skill II).','Explain respirators, including what N95 means.','Apply the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard, disinfection and waste handling in the laboratory.'],sections:[
['The chain of infection',[
'Infection spreads through a **chain of infection**: an **infectious agent** (bacteria, virus, fungus, parasite), a **reservoir** where it lives, a **portal of exit**, a **mode of transmission**, a **portal of entry** and a **susceptible host**. Breaking any link prevents infection. Laboratories break the chain with hand hygiene, PPE, engineering controls such as biological safety cabinets and sharps containers, disinfection and vaccination of workers against hepatitis B.',
order('Links in the chain of infection',['Infectious agent','Reservoir','Portal of exit','Mode of transmission','Portal of entry','Susceptible host'])]],
['Standard and transmission-based precautions',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to **all patients and specimens**, treating all blood, body fluids (except sweat), nonintact skin and mucous membranes as potentially infectious. They include hand hygiene, PPE based on expected exposure, safe sharps handling, respiratory hygiene and cleaning of equipment. **Transmission-based precautions** are added for known or suspected infections. **Contact precautions** (gown and gloves) prevent spread by touch, as for MRSA or *C. difficile*. **Droplet precautions** (surgical mask within close range) protect against large droplets, as for influenza or pertussis. **Airborne precautions** (fit-tested N95 respirator and an airborne infection isolation room with negative pressure) protect against small particles that remain in the air, as for tuberculosis, measles and chickenpox.',
sort('Which precaution type?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['MRSA wound infection',0],['Clostridioides difficile diarrhea',0],['Influenza',1],['Pertussis (whooping cough)',1],['Pulmonary tuberculosis',2],['Measles',2]])]],
['Respirators and the meaning of N95',[
'A **surgical mask** protects others from the wearer’s droplets and protects the wearer from splashes, but fits loosely. An **N95 respirator** is certified by NIOSH to filter **at least 95% of airborne particles** (tested with particles about 0.3 microns in size) when properly fitted; the “N” means it is **not resistant to oil**. N95s require **fit testing** and a **user seal check** each time they are put on. Laboratories use N95s or higher protection, along with biological safety cabinets, when working with organisms such as *Mycobacterium tuberculosis*.',
{t:'check',q:'What does the 95 in N95 respirator mean?',options:['It filters at least 95% of airborne particles','It is 95% effective against all viruses','It can be worn for 95 hours','It is 95% oil resistant'],why:'NIOSH certification requires at least 95% filtration of test particles; N means not oil resistant.'}]],
['Hand hygiene and PPE sequence (HOSA Skill II)',[
'HOSA Skill II begins by assembling PPE (gloves, mask and gown) and **removing jewelry**. For **handwashing**, the competitor turns on warm water using a paper towel on the faucet and discards it, applies soap and rubs fronts and backs of hands and between fingers vigorously for **15–30 seconds**, rinses with **fingertips pointed downward**, dries with a clean towel, uses the towel to turn off the faucet and discards it touching only the clean side. For **alcohol-based handrub**, the competitor covers all hand surfaces and rubs for at least 15 seconds until hands are completely dry. Donning follows a set order: **gown**, then **mask**, then **gloves** pulled over the gown cuffs (eyewear or face shields, when used, go on after the mask).',
'Removal is ordered to avoid contaminating yourself: untie the **gown’s waist ties**, remove **gloves** by turning them inside out without touching the outside and discard them, untie the **gown neck ties**, pull the gown down from the neck and fold it inside out while touching only the inside, discard it, then remove the **mask** by untying the bottom ties then the top (or lifting from the ears) and handling only the ties, and finally **perform hand hygiene**. The CDC’s published sequence offers an alternative order for removal; in the HOSA event, follow the rating sheet.',
order('Donning and doffing in HOSA Skill II order',['Perform hand hygiene','Put on the gown and tie it','Put on the mask','Put on gloves over the gown cuffs','Untie the gown waist ties','Remove gloves inside out and discard','Untie the neck ties and remove the gown inside out','Remove the mask by the ties','Perform hand hygiene'])]],
['Bloodborne pathogens, disinfection and waste',[
'OSHA’s **Bloodborne Pathogens Standard** protects workers from **HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C**. It requires an exposure control plan, training, engineering controls such as safety needles and sharps containers, PPE at no cost, **hepatitis B vaccine offered free**, and post-exposure evaluation. Never recap needles by hand; activate safety devices and discard sharps immediately in a puncture-resistant container. Work surfaces are cleaned before and after use with an appropriate disinfectant; a freshly prepared **10% bleach solution** (1:10 dilution of household bleach) is a common choice—another HOSA sample question. Biohazardous waste goes in red bags or labeled containers; some laboratories decontaminate it in an autoclave.',
'If an exposure occurs, such as a needlestick or a splash to the eyes, wash the skin with soap and water or flush mucous membranes and eyes at the **eyewash station** for at least 15 minutes, report immediately and seek post-exposure evaluation, which may include testing and preventive treatment.',
kase('A splash in the laboratory',{a:{text:'While uncapping a tube, a technician feels a splash of simulated blood near the eye. What is the first action?',choices:[['Flush the eye at the eyewash station for at least 15 minutes','b','Correct. Immediate flushing reduces exposure.',true],['Finish the run, then report it','b','Delaying first aid increases risk.',false]]},b:{text:'After flushing, what next?',choices:[['Report the exposure and get post-exposure evaluation','c','Correct. Reporting allows testing and treatment and documents the event.',true],['Keep it quiet if it seems minor','c','All exposures must be reported.',false]]},c:{text:'The technician is evaluated, the event is documented, and the lab reviews the use of acrylic shields and face protection when uncapping tubes.',end:true}})]],
["Chemical, physical and fire safety",["Laboratories also contain chemical and physical hazards. **Safety Data Sheets** describe each chemical’s hazards and handling; containers are labeled with GHS pictograms. Volatile or toxic chemicals such as formalin, xylene and acids are used in a **fume hood**, which draws vapors away from the worker. When diluting acid, **add acid to water**, never water to acid, to avoid violent spattering. Store flammables in approved cabinets and keep incompatible chemicals apart.","For fire, use the **RACE** response—**Rescue** anyone in danger, **Alarm** (activate the alarm), **Contain** the fire by closing doors, and **Extinguish** or evacuate—and operate an extinguisher with **PASS**: **Pull** the pin, **Aim** at the base of the fire, **Squeeze** the handle and **Sweep** side to side. Electrical safety means inspecting cords, never using damaged equipment and keeping liquids away from outlets. Know the locations of the **safety shower**, **eyewash station**, fire extinguishers, spill kits and exits before working."]]
],takeaways:['Break the chain of infection; standard precautions apply to every specimen.','Contact (gown, gloves), droplet (mask) and airborne (fit-tested N95, negative pressure) precautions are added by transmission route.','Skill II: wash 15–30 s fingertips down; don gown, mask, gloves; remove waist ties, gloves, neck ties and gown, mask, then hand hygiene; N95 filters at least 95% of particles; clean with 10% bleach.'],terms:[['Standard precautions','Infection-control practices used with all patients and specimens.'],['N95 respirator','Fit-tested respirator filtering at least 95% of airborne particles.'],['Bloodborne pathogen','Microorganism in blood that can cause disease, such as HIV or hepatitis B.']],mcqs:[q('What does 95 mean in N95 respirators?','Removes at least 95% of airborne particles','95% effective against all viruses','Can be reused 95 times','95% oil resistant','This is the HOSA sample question.'),q('Which solution is a common disinfectant for laboratory work surfaces?','10% chlorine bleach','40% alcohol','2% iodine','50% formaldehyde','This is a HOSA sample question.'),q('Tuberculosis requires which precautions?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','Standard only','TB spreads by small airborne particles.'),q('During handwashing, fingertips should point:','Downward','Upward','Sideways','It does not matter','Water flows away from clean hands.'),q('In HOSA Skill II, which PPE item is donned last?','Gloves','Gown','Mask','Hair cover','Gloves go over the gown cuffs.'),q('Which bloodborne pathogen has a vaccine employers must offer free?','Hepatitis B','HIV','Hepatitis C','Syphilis','OSHA requires the hepatitis B vaccine be offered.'),q('Standard precautions apply to:','All patients and specimens','Only patients with known infections','Only blood','Only surgical patients','Every specimen is treated as potentially infectious.'),q('How long should an eye be flushed after a splash?','At least 15 minutes','10 seconds','1 minute','Until it stops stinging','Prolonged flushing removes contaminants.')],frq:{prompt:'A technician must process specimens from a patient with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Explain the precautions required, the hand hygiene and PPE sequence from HOSA Skill II, and how the laboratory protects workers under the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard.',rubric:[['Airborne precautions and N95',['airborne','n95','95%','fit','negative pressure']],['Hand hygiene technique',['15','30 seconds','fingertips','paper towel','handrub']],['Don and doff order',['gown','mask','gloves','inside out','neck ties']],['Bloodborne pathogens protections',['hepatitis b','sharps','exposure control','10% bleach','report']]],model:'Suspected tuberculosis requires airborne precautions: a fit-tested N95 respirator that filters at least 95% of airborne particles, work in a biological safety cabinet and negative-pressure isolation for the patient. I would remove jewelry, wash hands with warm water and soap for 15–30 seconds with fingertips down, using a paper towel to operate the faucet, then put on a gown, mask or respirator and gloves over the gown cuffs. To remove PPE I would untie the waist ties, remove gloves inside out, untie the neck ties and peel the gown off inside out, remove the mask by its ties and perform hand hygiene. Under the Bloodborne Pathogens Standard, the laboratory provides an exposure control plan, training, safety sharps and containers, free PPE and free hepatitis B vaccination, disinfects surfaces with 10% bleach and requires immediate reporting of exposures.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_ISO,CDC_PPE,NIOSH_N95,OSHA_BBP]};

export const C3={title:'Laboratory math and the microscope',hook:'A white cell count reported as 8 instead of 8,000 per microliter, or a slide crushed by a 40x objective, are both avoidable mistakes. Laboratory math keeps units and dilutions straight, and correct microscope technique protects both the slide and the result.',objectives:['Convert metric units and calculate dilutions, concentrations and dilution factors.','Apply temperature conversions and significant figures in laboratory reports.','Identify microscope parts and calculate total magnification.','Focus a specimen at low and high power following HOSA Skill III.'],sections:[
['Units and conversions',[
'Laboratory results use metric units and prefixes: **kilo** (10³), **deci** (10⁻¹), **centi** (10⁻²), **milli** (10⁻³), **micro** (10⁻⁶) and **nano** (10⁻⁹). Common volume units are liters (L), deciliters (dL), milliliters (mL) and microliters (µL); 1 L = 10 dL = 1,000 mL and 1 mL = 1,000 µL. Many chemistry results are reported per deciliter (glucose in mg/dL) and cell counts per microliter (white cells per µL). Temperature converts with °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and °F = (°C × 9/5) + 32; incubators for human pathogens run at 35–37 °C (95–98.6 °F), and refrigerators for reagents at 2–8 °C.',
match('Match the equivalent',[['1 L','1,000 mL'],['1 mL','1,000 µL'],['1 dL','100 mL'],['1 g','1,000 mg'],['1 mg','1,000 µg'],['37 °C','98.6 °F']])]],
['Dilutions and dilution factors',[
'A **dilution** reduces concentration. A dilution described as **1:10** (or 1/10) in the laboratory means **1 part sample in 10 total parts**—for example, 0.1 mL of serum plus 0.9 mL of diluent. The **dilution factor** is the reciprocal: 10. To find the original concentration from a diluted result, **multiply by the dilution factor**. **Serial dilutions** multiply: a 1:10 dilution followed by another 1:10 gives 1:100. In serology, **titers** are reported as the highest dilution still giving a positive reaction, such as 1:64, from a twofold series (1:2, 1:4, 1:8...).',
'**C₁V₁ = C₂V₂** prepares solutions from stocks. Percent solutions (% w/v) are grams per 100 mL. Report results with appropriate **significant figures** and units, and round only the final answer.',
{t:'example',title:'Correct a diluted result',prompt:'A glucose result is above the analyzer’s range, so the sample is diluted 1:5 and rerun, giving 220 mg/dL. What is the patient’s glucose?',steps:['A 1:5 dilution has a dilution factor of 5.','Original concentration = 220 × 5 = 1,100 mg/dL.'],answer:'1,100 mg/dL. Reporting 220 would miss a dangerously high value; dilution factors must always be applied and documented.'},
{t:'w',kind:'mathlab',mode:'dilution',title:'Prepare a working solution from a stock'}]],
['The compound microscope',[
'The **binocular bright-field microscope** uses visible light passing up through the specimen. Key parts: **oculars (eyepieces)**, usually 10x; the **revolving nosepiece** holding the **objectives**—commonly **4x (scanning)**, **10x (low power)**, **40x (high dry)** and **100x (oil immersion)**; the **stage** with clips or a mechanical stage; the **condenser**, which focuses light onto the specimen; the **iris diaphragm**, which controls the amount of light; the **light source**; and the **coarse** and **fine adjustment** knobs. **Total magnification** = ocular × objective: 10 × 10 = 100x; 10 × 40 = 400x; 10 × 100 = 1,000x. **Resolution** is the ability to distinguish two close objects; immersion oil improves resolution at 100x by reducing light refraction. **Parfocal** objectives stay nearly in focus when switched.',
match('Microscope parts and functions',[['Focuses light onto the specimen','Condenser'],['Controls the amount of light','Iris diaphragm'],['Large focusing movements at low power','Coarse adjustment'],['Precise focusing, the only knob used at high power','Fine adjustment'],['Holds the objectives and rotates them','Revolving nosepiece'],['Lens you look through, usually 10x','Ocular']])]],
['Focusing a slide (HOSA Skill III)',[
'HOSA Skill III scores each step. After hand hygiene and assembling materials, the competitor **cleans the eyepiece and objectives with lens paper** (never tissue), raises the nosepiece with the coarse adjustment if applicable, **raises the condenser**, rotates the **10x objective** into position over the condenser, turns on the light and **opens the iris diaphragm** for maximum light. They place and secure the slide **specimen side up**, position the condenser nearly touching the slide, and—**looking at the stage from the side**—use the coarse adjustment to bring the objective as close to the slide as it will go. Then, looking through the oculars, they turn the coarse adjustment the opposite way to move the objective away until the specimen comes into focus, then switch to the **fine adjustment** for the sharpest focus; the judge confirms focus. To move to **40x**, they rotate the high-power objective while **watching from the side** so it does not strike the slide, then use **only the fine adjustment** to refocus. Afterward, they lower the stage, remove the slide, clean the lenses, turn off the light and store the microscope.',
order('Focus a specimen (HOSA Skill III)',['Clean oculars and objectives with lens paper','Raise the condenser and rotate the 10x objective into place','Turn on the light and open the iris diaphragm','Secure the slide specimen side up','Watching from the side, bring the objective close to the slide with coarse adjustment','Looking through the oculars, back away with coarse adjustment until in focus','Sharpen with the fine adjustment','Rotate to 40x while watching from the side, then refocus with fine adjustment only'])]],
['Care and troubleshooting',[
'Carry a microscope with **two hands**, one on the arm and one under the base. Never use the coarse adjustment at high power, because the objective can crash into the slide. Use oil only with the 100x objective and clean it off with lens paper afterward. If the image is dark, open the diaphragm or raise the condenser; if it is washed out and low in contrast, close the diaphragm slightly. If nothing appears, return to low power and center the specimen. **Electron microscopes**—transmission (TEM) for internal structure and scanning (SEM) for surface detail—magnify far beyond light microscopes but require special specimen preparation.',
kase('Nothing in focus',{a:{text:'At 40x the field is blurry and you cannot find the cells. What should you do?',choices:[['Return to 10x, center and focus the specimen, then switch back to 40x and use fine adjustment','b','Correct. Locate and center at low power first.',true],['Turn the coarse adjustment rapidly at 40x','b','This risks crashing the objective into the slide.',false]]},b:{text:'At 40x the image is very dark. What adjustment helps?',choices:[['Open the iris diaphragm or raise the condenser','c','Correct. Higher magnification needs more light.',true],['Close the diaphragm completely','c','That makes the image darker.',false]]},c:{text:'The cells come into sharp focus at 400x total magnification.',end:true}})]],
["Centrifuges, balances and pH",["**Centrifuges** separate components by spinning. Tubes must be **balanced** with tubes of equal volume and weight placed directly opposite each other, lids closed before starting and the rotor allowed to stop completely before opening. Speed is described in revolutions per minute (rpm), but the force applied is the **relative centrifugal force (RCF)** in multiples of gravity (× g), which depends on both speed and rotor radius. A **clinical centrifuge** separates serum or plasma, a **microhematocrit centrifuge** packs cells in capillary tubes, and a **serological centrifuge** spins small tubes briefly in blood banking.","A **pH meter** measures acidity with an electrode; it is calibrated with standard **buffers** (commonly pH 4, 7 and 10) before use, rinsed with distilled water between samples and stored in storage solution. **pH indicator strips** give quick estimates. A **top-loading balance** weighs to about 0.01 g, while an **analytical balance** weighs to 0.0001 g inside a draft shield; both are tared before weighing. Recording the instrument and calibration status in the log supports quality."]]
],takeaways:['1 L = 1,000 mL; 1 mL = 1,000 µL; multiply diluted results by the dilution factor; 1:10 means 1 part in 10 total.','Total magnification = ocular × objective (10x × 40x = 400x); oil immersion is 100x objective.','Skill III: lens paper, condenser up, 10x, light and diaphragm, watch from the side, coarse then fine, 40x with fine only.'],terms:[['Dilution factor','Reciprocal of the dilution; multiply by it to get original concentration.'],['Total magnification','Ocular magnification times objective magnification.'],['Parfocal','Objectives that stay nearly in focus when switched.']],mcqs:[q('What is the total magnification with a 10x ocular and 40x objective?','400x','50x','40x','4,000x','Multiply ocular by objective.'),q('A 1:10 dilution in the laboratory means:','1 part sample in 10 total parts','1 part sample plus 10 parts diluent','10 parts sample in 1 part diluent','A 10% error','Total parts equals 10.'),q('A sample diluted 1:4 reads 75 mg/dL. The original concentration is:','300 mg/dL','75 mg/dL','18.75 mg/dL','79 mg/dL','Multiply by the dilution factor 4.'),q('What should be used to clean microscope lenses?','Lens paper','Paper towels','Facial tissue','Your shirt','Lens paper avoids scratches.'),q('At high power (40x), which knob should be used to focus?','Fine adjustment only','Coarse adjustment','Both rapidly','The condenser knob','Coarse focus can crash the objective.'),q('Which part controls the amount of light reaching the specimen?','Iris diaphragm','Ocular','Stage clips','Nosepiece','The condenser focuses light; the diaphragm regulates it.'),q('How many microliters are in 1 milliliter?','1,000','100','10','1,000,000','Micro is 10⁻⁶; milli is 10⁻³.'),q('When bringing the objective close to the slide with coarse adjustment, you should:','Watch from the side','Look through the oculars','Close your eyes','Use the 100x objective','Watching prevents crashing into the slide.')],frq:{prompt:'A blood smear must be examined at 400x. Describe how to set up and focus the microscope following HOSA Skill III, calculate total magnification at each objective, and explain how you would correct a result from a sample diluted 1:10 that reads 4.2 on the instrument.',rubric:[['Setup steps',['lens paper','condenser','10x','diaphragm','specimen side up']],['Focusing technique',['side','coarse','fine','40x','strike']],['Magnification calculations',['40x','100x','400x','1,000x','ocular']],['Dilution correction',['42','dilution factor','10','multiply']]],model:'I would clean the oculars and objectives with lens paper, raise the condenser, rotate the 10x objective into place, turn on the light and open the iris diaphragm, then secure the slide specimen side up. Watching from the side, I would use the coarse adjustment to bring the objective close to the slide, then look through the oculars and back it away until the cells come into focus, sharpening with the fine adjustment. To reach 400x, I would rotate the 40x objective while watching from the side so it does not strike the slide and refocus using only the fine adjustment. With 10x oculars, total magnification is 40x with the 4x objective, 100x with 10x, 400x with 40x and 1,000x with the 100x oil objective. A 1:10 diluted sample reading 4.2 must be multiplied by the dilution factor of 10, giving 42.'},sources:[RULES,['NIST: metric SI prefixes','https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si-prefixes'],BLS_LAB]};

export const C4={title:'Hematology: blood cells, the CBC and blood smears',hook:'A single drop of blood spread thin on a glass slide and stained purple and pink can reveal anemia, infection, leukemia or malaria. Hematology studies the cells of the blood, and HOSA tests both the knowledge behind a complete blood count and the hands-on skills of making and staining a blood smear.',objectives:['Describe the composition of blood and the formation of blood cells.','Interpret the main components of a complete blood count, including RBC indices and the differential.','Make a blood smear following HOSA Skill V-A.','Stain a blood smear with Wright’s stain following HOSA Skill V-B.'],sections:[
['Composition of blood',[
'Blood is about **55% plasma** and **45% formed elements**. **Plasma** is mostly water carrying proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen), electrolytes, nutrients, hormones and wastes. **Serum** is plasma without clotting factors—the liquid left after blood clots. Formed elements are **red blood cells (erythrocytes)**, **white blood cells (leukocytes)** and **platelets (thrombocytes)**, all produced in the **red bone marrow** by **hematopoiesis** from stem cells. When a tube of anticoagulated blood is centrifuged, it separates into plasma on top, a thin **buffy coat** of white cells and platelets, and packed red cells at the bottom; the red cell percentage is the **hematocrit**.',
'**Red blood cells** are biconcave, have no nucleus when mature, contain **hemoglobin** to carry oxygen and live about **120 days** before removal by the spleen. **Erythropoietin** from the kidneys stimulates their production. **Reticulocytes** are young red cells; a high count shows the marrow is responding to blood loss or destruction.']],
['The complete blood count',[
'The **complete blood count (CBC)** is one of the most ordered tests. It includes the **RBC count**, **hemoglobin (Hgb)**, **hematocrit (Hct)**, **RBC indices**, **WBC count**, **platelet count** and often a **differential**. Typical adult reference ranges (which vary by laboratory) are: hemoglobin about 13.5–17.5 g/dL for men and 12.0–15.5 g/dL for women; hematocrit about 41–50% for men and 36–44% for women; WBC about 4,500–11,000/µL; and platelets about 150,000–450,000/µL. As a rule of thumb, hematocrit is roughly three times the hemoglobin.',
'**RBC indices** describe red cells: **mean corpuscular volume (MCV)**, the average size (normal about 80–100 fL; low = **microcytic**, as in iron deficiency; high = **macrocytic**, as in vitamin B12 or folate deficiency); **mean corpuscular hemoglobin (MCH)**; and **mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC)**, which reflects color (low = **hypochromic**). **Anemia** is a decrease in red cells or hemoglobin; **polycythemia** is an increase. **Leukocytosis** is a high WBC count, often from infection; **leukopenia** is low. **Thrombocytopenia** is a low platelet count, raising bleeding risk.',
match('Match the CBC term',[['Average red cell size','MCV'],['Percentage of blood volume that is red cells','Hematocrit'],['Small red cells, as in iron deficiency','Microcytic'],['High white cell count','Leukocytosis'],['Low platelet count','Thrombocytopenia'],['Young red cells showing marrow response','Reticulocytes']])]],
['White cells and the differential',[
'The **differential** reports the percentage of each white cell type. **Neutrophils** (about 50–70%) have multilobed nuclei and fight bacterial infection; immature **band** forms increase in acute infection (a “left shift”). **Lymphocytes** (about 20–40%) include B and T cells and rise in many viral infections. **Monocytes** (about 2–8%) are the largest white cells and become macrophages. **Eosinophils** (about 1–4%) have red-orange granules and rise in allergies and parasitic infections. **Basophils** (under 1%) have dark blue-black granules and release histamine. The memory aid “**Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas**” orders them from most to least common. A **manual differential cell counter** tallies 100 cells as the technologist identifies each on a stained smear.',
sort('Which white cell?',['Neutrophil','Lymphocyte','Eosinophil','Basophil','Monocyte'],[['Most common; multilobed nucleus; bacterial infection',0],['B and T cells; often increase in viral infection',1],['Red-orange granules; allergies and parasites',2],['Dark granules; releases histamine; rarest',3],['Largest white cell; becomes a macrophage',4]])]],
['Making a blood smear (HOSA Skill V-A)',[
'A good smear has a smooth **feathered edge** where cells lie in a single layer. In Skill V-A, the competitor assembles materials, performs hand hygiene and puts on **gloves, gown, mask or face shield and protective eyewear**. They clean the slide and coverslip or spreader with an alcohol swab and **check both for chips and defects**. They place a **2 mm drop** of blood (about the size of a match head) about **¾ inch from the end** of the slide, centered. They place the spreader’s edge in front of the drop, hold it at a **30–35° angle**, pull it back until it touches the blood, and wait while the blood spreads along the edge. With a **firm, steady, continuous motion** they push the spreader to the opposite end, keeping contact with the slide, and finish by lifting in a smooth, low arc. The spreader goes into the **sharps container**. The slide **air-dries**; the judge verifies the smear is about **1½ inches long, smooth, thin and with even margins**. The slide is labeled with the patient’s name, doctor’s name and ID number, the area is cleaned and disinfected, PPE removed and hands washed.',
'Common problems: a smear that is too thick and short (angle too steep or drop too large), too thin and long (angle too shallow), streaked or with holes (dirty slide or uneven pressure) or with no feathered edge (pushing too fast or lifting early).',
order('Make a blood smear (HOSA Skill V-A)',['Hand hygiene and full PPE','Clean and inspect slide and spreader','Place a 2 mm drop about ¾ inch from the end','Hold the spreader at 30–35° in front of the drop','Pull back to touch the drop and let it spread','Push to the end in one smooth, continuous motion','Discard the spreader in sharps and air-dry the slide','Label, clean, remove PPE and wash hands'])]],
['Staining the smear (HOSA Skill V-B)',[
'**Wright’s stain** is a Romanowsky stain containing **methylene blue** (stains acidic structures such as nuclei blue-purple) and **eosin** (stains basic structures such as hemoglobin and eosinophil granules pink-orange). In Skill V-B, after hand hygiene and gloves (plus gown, mask or face shield and eyewear if splashing is possible), the competitor places the **dry smear side up on a level staining rack**, **completely covers it with Wright’s stain while counting the drops aloud**, and verbalizes leaving it for **1–3 minutes**. They then add **distilled water or buffer one drop at a time in an amount equal to the stain**, **blowing gently** along the slide to mix, and verbalize standing **2–4 minutes**; the judge looks for an **oily, green metallic sheen** showing proper mixing. Finally they wash the slide by gently flooding with distilled water, clean and replace equipment, disinfect the counter and complete hand hygiene.',
kase('A poorly stained smear',{a:{text:'Under the microscope, all cells look too blue and red cells look gray-blue. What is a likely cause?',choices:[['Buffer too alkaline, stain left too long or inadequate washing','b','Correct. Overly blue smears suggest these problems.',true],['The smear was too thin','b','Thickness affects distribution, not overall color balance.',false]]},b:{text:'On the next smear, red cells look bright red and white cell nuclei are pale. What does this suggest?',choices:[['Buffer too acidic or excessive washing','c','Correct. Overly pink smears suggest acidic buffer or overwashing.',true],['Perfect staining','c','Nuclei should be clearly purple.',false]]},c:{text:'With correct timing, equal buffer and gentle washing, nuclei stain purple, red cells pink-tan and eosinophil granules orange.',end:true}})]],
["Microhematocrit and manual cell counts",["The **microhematocrit** measures the packed cell volume. Blood is drawn into a capillary tube about three-quarters full, one end is sealed with **sealant clay**, and the tube is placed in a **microhematocrit centrifuge** with the sealed end toward the outside and balanced with another tube. After spinning, the tube shows packed red cells at the bottom, a thin buffy coat and plasma on top. A reading device or chart measures the percentage of the total column occupied by red cells. Errors include insufficient centrifugation, clots and reading the buffy coat as red cells.","Manual cell counts use a **hemacytometer**, a thick glass slide with a precisely ruled counting chamber of known volume. Blood is diluted with a fluid that lyses red cells (for white counts) or preserves them (for red counts), the chamber is filled by capillary action under a coverslip, cells are counted in specified squares under the microscope, and the count is multiplied by the dilution and volume factors to give cells per microliter. Automated hematology analyzers now perform most counts, but manual methods remain important for body fluids and verifying unusual results."]]
],takeaways:['Blood is about 55% plasma and 45% cells; serum lacks clotting factors; RBCs live about 120 days.','CBC includes Hgb, Hct, MCV, WBC and platelets; differential order: neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils.','Skill V-A: 2 mm drop, ¾ inch from end, 30–35° spreader, smooth push, 1½ inch smear; Skill V-B: cover with Wright’s stain 1–3 min, equal buffer 2–4 min, green sheen, rinse.'],terms:[['Hematocrit','Percentage of blood volume made up of red cells.'],['Differential','Percentages of each white cell type.'],['Wright’s stain','Romanowsky stain of methylene blue and eosin for blood smears.']],mcqs:[q('Plasma makes up about what percentage of blood?','55%','45%','10%','90%','Formed elements are about 45%.'),q('Serum differs from plasma because serum lacks:','Clotting factors','Water','Electrolytes','Glucose','Serum remains after clotting.'),q('The most common white blood cell in adults is the:','Neutrophil','Lymphocyte','Monocyte','Basophil','Neutrophils are about 50–70%.'),q('Which white cells increase in allergies and parasitic infections?','Eosinophils','Neutrophils','Basophils only','Monocytes','They have red-orange granules.'),q('Red blood cells live about:','120 days','12 days','12 hours','1 year','They are removed by the spleen.'),q('In Skill V-A, at what angle is the spreader slide held?','30–35°','90°','10°','60°','This produces an even smear.'),q('In Skill V-B, what indicates proper mixing of stain and buffer?','An oily, green metallic sheen','Bubbles','A red color','A clear slide','The judge verifies the sheen.'),q('A low MCV with pale cells is typical of:','Iron deficiency anemia','Vitamin B12 deficiency','Polycythemia','Leukocytosis','Microcytic, hypochromic cells suggest iron deficiency.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient’s CBC shows low hemoglobin and a low MCV. Explain what these results suggest, describe how you would prepare and stain a blood smear following HOSA Skills V-A and V-B, and describe what you would look for on the smear.',rubric:[['Interpret CBC',['anemia','microcytic','iron','mcv','hemoglobin']],['Smear preparation steps',['2 mm','¾ inch','30','35','smooth','air-dry']],['Staining steps',['wright','1-3','1–3','buffer','2-4','2–4','sheen','rinse']],['What to examine',['red cells','size','color','white','differential','platelet']]],model:'Low hemoglobin indicates anemia, and a low MCV means the red cells are small (microcytic), which most commonly suggests iron deficiency. To make the smear, I would wear full PPE, clean and inspect the slide and spreader, place a 2 mm drop about ¾ inch from the end, hold the spreader at 30–35°, let the blood spread along its edge and push in one smooth, continuous motion, discard the spreader in sharps and air-dry the slide before labeling it. To stain, I would place the smear on a level rack, cover it with Wright’s stain counting drops aloud for 1–3 minutes, add an equal amount of buffer while blowing gently to mix for 2–4 minutes until a green metallic sheen appears, then rinse gently with distilled water. On the smear I would examine red cell size and color for small, pale cells, review white cells with a 100-cell differential and estimate platelets.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_CBC,MEDLINE_SMEAR]};

export const C5={title:'Hemostasis and coagulation testing',hook:'A small cut stops bleeding within minutes because of a precise sequence: the vessel narrows, platelets plug the hole and a mesh of fibrin seals it. When any step fails, a person may bleed too much; when it overreacts, dangerous clots form. Coagulation testing measures this balance and guides blood thinner therapy.',objectives:['Describe the stages of hemostasis and the role of platelets and clotting factors.','Explain the intrinsic, extrinsic and common pathways and fibrinolysis.','Interpret PT/INR, aPTT, platelet count, fibrinogen and D-dimer tests.','Describe specimen requirements and common bleeding and clotting disorders.'],sections:[
['Stages of hemostasis',[
'**Hemostasis** stops bleeding in overlapping stages. **Vascular spasm**: the injured vessel constricts. **Primary hemostasis**: **platelets** adhere to exposed collagen with the help of **von Willebrand factor**, become activated, release chemicals and aggregate into a **platelet plug**. **Secondary hemostasis**: the **coagulation cascade** activates clotting factors in sequence, converting **prothrombin** to **thrombin**, which converts soluble **fibrinogen** to insoluble **fibrin** strands that stabilize the plug. Finally, **fibrinolysis** dissolves the clot as tissue heals: **plasminogen** becomes **plasmin**, which breaks fibrin into **fibrin degradation products**, including **D-dimer**.',
order('Order the events of hemostasis',['Vascular spasm narrows the vessel','Platelets adhere with von Willebrand factor and aggregate into a plug','Coagulation cascade generates thrombin','Thrombin converts fibrinogen to fibrin, stabilizing the clot','Plasmin breaks down fibrin during healing, releasing D-dimer'])]],
['The coagulation pathways',[
'Clotting factors, mostly proteins made by the **liver**, are numbered with Roman numerals. The classic model describes three pathways. The **extrinsic pathway** begins when **tissue factor (factor III)** from damaged tissue activates **factor VII**. The **intrinsic pathway** begins with contact activation of **factor XII** and involves factors XI, IX and VIII. Both lead to the **common pathway**: factor **X**, factor **V**, prothrombin (**factor II**), and fibrinogen (**factor I**), with **factor XIII** cross-linking fibrin. **Calcium (factor IV)** is required throughout, which is why anticoagulants in collection tubes work by binding calcium. **Vitamin K** is needed to make factors II, VII, IX and X; warfarin works by blocking vitamin K.',
match('Match the factor or term',[['Factor I','Fibrinogen'],['Factor II','Prothrombin'],['Factor III','Tissue factor'],['Factor IV','Calcium'],['Deficient in hemophilia A','Factor VIII'],['Deficient in hemophilia B','Factor IX']])]],
['Coagulation tests',[
'The **prothrombin time (PT)** measures the **extrinsic and common pathways**; it is reported with the **international normalized ratio (INR)** to standardize results across laboratories and is used to **monitor warfarin**. A typical INR is about 1.0 without anticoagulation, and therapeutic targets on warfarin are often 2.0–3.0 depending on the condition. The **activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)** measures the **intrinsic and common pathways** and traditionally monitors **unfractionated heparin**; it is prolonged in hemophilia. The **platelet count** and **platelet function tests** (such as a **platelet aggregation profiler**) assess primary hemostasis. **Fibrinogen** levels fall when clotting factors are consumed. **D-dimer** rises when clots are being broken down, helping rule out **deep vein thrombosis**, **pulmonary embolism** or **disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)**; a **rapid latex agglutination test for D-dimer** is on HOSA’s instrument list. **Point-of-care coagulation analyzers** measure PT/INR from a fingerstick for patients on warfarin.',
sort('Which test best answers the question?',['PT/INR','aPTT','D-dimer','Platelet count'],[['Is this patient’s warfarin dose in range?',0],['Is the extrinsic pathway prolonged?',0],['Is unfractionated heparin therapy in range?',1],['Could this patient have hemophilia A?',1],['Could this patient have a pulmonary embolism?',2],['Is there evidence of clot breakdown in DIC?',2],['Why is this patient bruising after chemotherapy?',3]])]],
['Specimen collection for coagulation',[
'Coagulation tests use a **light blue–top tube** containing **3.2% sodium citrate**, which binds calcium. The tube must be filled to the correct level to keep a **9:1 blood-to-anticoagulant ratio**; underfilled tubes give falsely prolonged results. Tubes are gently inverted 3–4 times to mix, never shaken. In the order of draw, the light blue tube comes after blood cultures and before serum, heparin, EDTA and gray tubes, so additives from other tubes do not contaminate it. When a winged collection set is used and the light blue tube is first, a **discard tube** is drawn to fill the tubing’s dead space. Samples are tested promptly or processed to platelet-poor plasma.',
order('Common order of draw',['Blood culture bottles','Light blue (sodium citrate) for coagulation','Red or gold (serum, clot activator, gel)','Green (heparin)','Lavender (EDTA) for hematology','Gray (sodium fluoride/potassium oxalate) for glucose'])]],
['Bleeding and clotting disorders',[
'**Hemophilia A** (factor VIII deficiency) and **hemophilia B** (factor IX deficiency) are X-linked inherited disorders affecting mostly males and causing joint and muscle bleeding; aPTT is prolonged while PT is normal. **Von Willebrand disease** is the most common inherited bleeding disorder, affecting platelet adhesion and factor VIII levels, with nosebleeds and heavy menstrual bleeding. **Thrombocytopenia** from marrow failure, immune destruction or medications causes petechiae and bleeding. **Vitamin K deficiency** and **liver disease** prolong PT. On the clotting side, **deep vein thrombosis** and **pulmonary embolism** involve unwanted clots; **DIC** combines widespread clotting with bleeding as factors and platelets are consumed, showing prolonged PT and aPTT, low platelets and fibrinogen, and high D-dimer.',
kase('A prolonged clotting time',{a:{text:'A 10-year-old boy has swollen, painful knees after minor falls. PT is normal; aPTT is prolonged; platelets are normal. Which condition fits best?',choices:[['Hemophilia (intrinsic pathway factor deficiency)','b','Correct. Isolated prolonged aPTT with joint bleeding suggests hemophilia.',true],['Warfarin effect','b','Warfarin prolongs PT/INR.',false]]},b:{text:'Which follow-up test would identify the specific type?',choices:[['Factor VIII and factor IX assays','c','Correct. These distinguish hemophilia A from B.',true],['A urinalysis','c','Urinalysis does not measure clotting factors.',false]]},c:{text:'Factor VIII is very low, confirming hemophilia A. The family receives genetic counseling because the condition is X-linked.',end:true}})]],
["Antithrombotic drugs and platelet testing",["Many patients take drugs that affect hemostasis. **Antiplatelet drugs** such as **aspirin** and **clopidogrel** reduce platelet aggregation to prevent heart attacks and strokes; their effect can be assessed with **platelet aggregation studies** on a **platelet aggregation profiler**. **Anticoagulants** include **warfarin** (monitored with PT/INR), **unfractionated heparin** (monitored with aPTT or anti-Xa testing), **low-molecular-weight heparin** and **direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs)** such as apixaban and rivaroxaban, which usually do not require routine monitoring. **Thrombolytic** drugs dissolve existing clots in emergencies such as stroke.","Bleeding risk rises when these drugs are combined or when dosing is too high, and reversal agents exist for several of them (for example, vitamin K for warfarin and protamine for heparin). Laboratory professionals support safe therapy by producing accurate, timely coagulation results, flagging critical values such as very high INRs, and ensuring specimens are collected correctly, since a heparin-contaminated sample drawn from a line can falsely prolong the aPTT."]]
],takeaways:['Hemostasis: vascular spasm, platelet plug (von Willebrand factor), coagulation cascade to fibrin, then fibrinolysis releasing D-dimer.','PT/INR (extrinsic, warfarin) and aPTT (intrinsic, heparin, hemophilia); D-dimer for DVT, PE and DIC.','Light blue sodium citrate tube, filled 9:1, inverted gently; hemophilia A = factor VIII, B = factor IX; von Willebrand disease is most common.'],terms:[['Fibrin','Insoluble protein mesh that stabilizes a clot.'],['INR','International normalized ratio standardizing PT results.'],['D-dimer','Fibrin breakdown product indicating clot lysis.']],mcqs:[q('Which test is used to monitor warfarin therapy?','PT/INR','aPTT','Platelet count','D-dimer','Warfarin affects vitamin K–dependent factors.'),q('Which tube is used for coagulation testing?','Light blue (sodium citrate)','Lavender (EDTA)','Gray (sodium fluoride)','Green (heparin)','Citrate binds calcium reversibly.'),q('Hemophilia A is a deficiency of factor:','VIII','IX','VII','X','Hemophilia B is factor IX.'),q('Thrombin converts fibrinogen into:','Fibrin','Plasmin','Prothrombin','Platelets','Fibrin stabilizes the clot.'),q('An elevated D-dimer suggests:','Clot formation and breakdown, as in DVT or PE','Iron deficiency','Normal hemostasis only','Vitamin C deficiency','D-dimer comes from fibrin breakdown.'),q('The most common inherited bleeding disorder is:','Von Willebrand disease','Hemophilia A','Hemophilia B','DIC','It affects platelet adhesion.'),q('Why must a light blue tube be filled to the line?','To keep the 9:1 blood-to-anticoagulant ratio','To prevent hemolysis','To make it heavier','To change its color','Underfilling prolongs results.'),q('Which vitamin is needed to make factors II, VII, IX and X?','Vitamin K','Vitamin C','Vitamin D','Vitamin B12','Warfarin blocks vitamin K.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a blood clot forms and dissolves, then describe how you would collect and interpret coagulation tests for a patient on warfarin and a patient suspected of having hemophilia.',rubric:[['Hemostasis and fibrinolysis',['platelet','fibrin','thrombin','cascade','plasmin','d-dimer']],['Specimen requirements',['light blue','citrate','9:1','invert','order of draw']],['Warfarin monitoring',['pt','inr','vitamin k','extrinsic']],['Hemophilia interpretation',['aptt','factor viii','factor ix','intrinsic','normal pt']]],model:'After injury, the vessel constricts, platelets adhere with von Willebrand factor and form a plug, and the coagulation cascade generates thrombin, which converts fibrinogen to fibrin to stabilize the clot; later, plasmin breaks fibrin down, releasing D-dimer. Coagulation specimens go in a light blue sodium citrate tube filled to maintain a 9:1 ratio, inverted gently, drawn in the correct order of draw and tested promptly. Warfarin blocks vitamin K–dependent factors, so its effect is monitored with PT reported as INR, reflecting the extrinsic and common pathways, with typical targets around 2–3. A patient with suspected hemophilia would show a normal PT and prolonged aPTT because factor VIII or IX in the intrinsic pathway is deficient, and factor assays would identify hemophilia A or B.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_PT,NHLBI_BLEED]};
